    "image_with_text_ndNhdw": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_rTJXUT": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Are the Common Applications of Vinyl Tapes?",
            "highlight_style": "none",
            "highlight_font_style": "italic",
            "highlight_style_color": "",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_Ai7R3y": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A well-made vinyl tape can withstand moisture, chemicals, changing temperatures, and abrasions, making it one of the most durable choices available for rugged, demanding applications. Vinyl has several unique qualities that make it an ideal choice for a range of applications including:</p><ul><li>Sealing ducts and joints</li><li>Pipe wrapping and harness wrapping</li><li>Masking printed circuit boards</li><li>Color coding, safety coding, and lane marking</li><li>Decorating applications</li><li>Providing striping on vehicles or sporting equipment</li><li>Light bundling</li><li>Marking hazards at construction sites or in areas frequented by the public</li></ul><p><br/>Marking aisles, walkways, and egress or navigational routes, In addition to these applications, vinyl electrical tapes are used with materials that conduct electricity to insulate electrical wires. They are pressure-sensitive and very effective at preventing electrical currents from accidentally passing onto nearby wires. Vinyl electrical tapes are used in low-voltage household applications as well as workplace electrical applications, where they are used for sealing, protecting, and insulating wiring jobs, as well as for making repairs.</p>",
            "subtext_color": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_rTJXUT",
        "text_Ai7R3y"
      ],
      "disabled": true,
